## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design online dynamic order-statistics with batch inserts, focusing on tangible metrics (inversions) and practical data structures, not abstract theory.

## Key Concepts

- Inversions, Fenwick tree, coordinate compression, online batch processing, duplicates handling, memory trade-offs.

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i,delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
}
// assume valueToRank and rankToValue are defined, U is number of ranks
let bit = new Fenwick(U);
let totalInv = 0;
let seen = 0;
for (const x of batch){
  const r = valueToRank[x];
  totalInv += seen - bit.query(r);
  bit.update(r, 1);
  seen++;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle deletions or updates to previous elements?
- How would you adapt if the value domain is unbounded or values arrive online without prior compression?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of streaming quantiles and the trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and latency. It also evaluates familiarity with mergeable sketches for distributed systems.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming quantiles (TDigest/KLL)
- Mergeable data sketches across shards
- Compression parameter and accuracy vs memory trade-offs
- Update and query time complexities

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: TDigest usage
class TDigest { 
  add(x); 
  quantile(p); 
  merge(other); 
}

const digest = new TDigest(100);
for (const latency of batch) {
  digest.add(latency);
}
const p95 = digest.quantile(0.95);
```

## Performance Comparison
- **TDigest**: O(n log k) total time, O(k) memory, O(log k) per-element update
- **Recomputing**: O(n log n) total time, O(n) memory, O(1) per-element (but requires storage)

The streaming approach enables real-time 95th percentile computation with strict memory limits and supports distributed merging across shards.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests streaming algorithm intuition and amortized reasoning. Detecting heavy hitters with bounded memory on a growing stream is a common interview topic; Misra–Gries provides deterministic guarantees with simple updates.

## Key Concepts
- Misra–Gries heavy-hitters
- Choice of k = floor(1/phi) + 1 to bound missed items
- Amortized O(1) per element, O(k) memory
- Trade-offs: larger k reduces false positives but uses more memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class MGHeavyHitters {
  constructor(k) {
    this.k = k;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }
  update(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
      return;
    }
    if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
      return;
    }
    for (const [key, val] of Array.from(this.counters)) {
      if (val === 1) this.counters.delete(key);
      else this.counters.set(key, val - 1);
    }
  }
  candidates() { return Array.from(this.counters.keys()); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the phi-bound with a second pass or a secondary sketch in production?
- How does batch size B influence practical update throughput and memory usage?
